<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Day of the Dead</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Día de Muertos, Día de los Muertos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Day of the Dead is a holiday traditionally celebrated on 1 and 2 November, widely observed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and among people of Mexican heritage elsewhere, in which family and friends gather to remember those who have died. Families build home altars called ofrendas with the favourite foods and beverages of the departed, and visit graves with calaveras and marigold flowers. Mexican academics are divided on whether it has indigenous pre-Hispanic roots or is a Spanish tradition recast in the 20th century, under the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas">Lázaro Cárdenas</a>, to encourage Mexican nationalism. In 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/day-of-the-dead.jpg" alt="Day of the Dead" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RubeHM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_de_Día_de_Muertos_en_Actopan,_Hidalgo,_México_(2017)._24.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mesoamerican traditions live on in the religious life of Mexico and Central America: in the <b>Day of the Dead</b>, with its offerings to returning ancestors, in the cult of saints and sacred places, and in the ceremonies of Maya day-keepers, whose spirituality Guatemala recognised in the peace accords…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sad tone keeps it from being counted a love song, and across Latin America it is associated with the <b>Day of the Dead</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-llorona">La Llorona</a></li></ul></section>
